pfx incorporates ideas and, in two places, code from Omarchy
(https://github.com/basecamp/omarchy), which is released under the MIT License.

Copyright (c) Basecamp / David Heinemeier Hansson and Omarchy contributors.

Adapted with attribution:

  default/walker/themes/pfx/layout.xml
      Copied unchanged from Omarchy's walker theme. It is a plain GTK4 widget
      tree describing walker's window structure, with no styling opinions.

  bin/pfx-menu-keybindings  (xkb keycode-to-symbol mapping)
      The approach of compiling the active keymap with `xkbcli compile-keymap`
      and correlating xkb_keycodes against xkb_symbols is Omarchy's. The
      implementation here is rewritten and reads `hyprctl binds` plain text
      rather than the JSON output, which is broken on Hyprland 0.56.0.

Everything else is an independent implementation. The structural ideas that
pfx borrows deliberately — a replaceable system-defaults tree beside a sacred
user-config tree, web apps as generated .desktop entries, a dmenu-driven menu
tree, atomic theme swapping — are architecture, not code.
